WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.105
The policy library has five types, each
with an ID prefix: compliance policies, CP;

2
00:00:07.105 --> 00:00:11.488
settings catalog profiles,
SC; endpoint security,

3
00:00:11.488 --> 00:00:15.550
ES; app protection, AP;
and app configuration, AC.

4
00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:20.111
Every one is assigned to dynamic
device groups built from attributes,

5
00:00:20.111 --> 00:00:22.313
which replaces OU-linked scoping.

6
00:00:22.313 --> 00:00:27.354
Entra dynamic rules for devices can use
extension attributes one through fifteen,

7
00:00:27.354 --> 00:00:31.738
but the values have to be written to
the device object through Microsoft

8
00:00:31.738 --> 00:00:35.537
Graph first; they're the device's
own set, not the user attributes.

9
00:00:35.537 --> 00:00:39.879
The program uses one numbering: one
for region, three for department,

10
00:00:39.879 --> 00:00:43.212
eleven for security tier,
twelve for device role and

11
00:00:43.212 --> 00:00:47.088
thirteen for environment, with
one fixed spelling per value.

12
00:00:47.088 --> 00:00:51.055
Assignments then add exclude groups
for break-glass and staging,

13
00:00:51.055 --> 00:00:52.525
and assignment filters.

14
00:00:52.525 --> 00:00:56.887
The correction that matters: filters
can't read extension attributes.

15
00:00:56.887 --> 00:00:59.988
A filter on attribute
eleven doesn't evaluate.

16
00:00:59.988 --> 00:01:04.109
Attribute targeting belongs in
the group rule; filters work on

17
00:01:04.109 --> 00:01:08.549
Intune properties such as join type,
ownership, model and OS edition.

18
00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:11.912
Three Microsoft facts shape
every compliance template.

19
00:01:11.912 --> 00:01:14.983
Noncompliance actions
are scheduled in days,

20
00:01:14.983 --> 00:01:17.812
zero to three hundred
sixty-five, not hours.

21
00:01:17.812 --> 00:01:22.940
Windows supports only three actions:
mark noncompliant, email the user,

22
00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:27.112
and add to the retire list,
which retires nothing by itself.

23
00:01:27.112 --> 00:01:28.580
There is no block action;

24
00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:32.837
blocking is what Conditional Access
does with a noncompliant device.

25
00:01:32.837 --> 00:01:37.220
And BitLocker, Secure Boot and code
integrity are measured at boot,

26
00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.088
so a newly encrypted
device needs a restart.

27
00:01:40.088 --> 00:01:44.154
The set: a workstation
baseline with a 24H2 floor;

28
00:01:44.154 --> 00:01:47.406
a strict Tier 0 policy with a 25H2 floor,

29
00:01:47.406 --> 00:01:53.301
a Clear risk score and no grace period;
a Tier 2 policy; and a kiosk policy.

30
00:01:53.301 --> 00:02:00.720
The server baseline, CP-004, can't be
built in Intune, so it moves to Chapter 09.
